        <body>
            <div xml:id="d432">

                <pb facs="0378" n="366" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg366"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.7962/56: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wharton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-03-09T16:00:00-00:44">March 9, 1942—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 9—3:41 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>63. From McBride. Legation’s 56, March 6, 11 p.m. Pending arrival of nucleus
                    force, it is believed urgent that some protection be provided for Harbel Field
                    immediately. Colonel Fox, Captain Beard, Wharton, Firestone manager and I
                    suggest that Captain Beard be authorized to organize and instruct about 20
                    carefully selected Pan American construction men now on field in use of 2
                    machine guns which Colonel Kemp has made available from Accra. Weapons and
                    ammunition to remain in custody of two commissioned officers who are now here at
                    all times. This will not interfere with or delay Beard’s mission. [McBride.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wharton</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
